WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.501 --> 00:00:03.178
(loud whoosh)

00:00:03.178 --> 00:00:04.294
- What's up, Believe Nation?

00:00:04.294 --> 00:00:06.220
I started the Mentor
Me series with the goal

00:00:06.220 --> 00:00:09.444
to try to learn from people
who've done a lot more than us

00:00:09.444 --> 00:00:10.930
and leverage some of their skills,

00:00:10.930 --> 00:00:11.995
their knowledge, their ideas,

00:00:11.995 --> 00:00:14.781
to help us become the
best version of ourselves.

00:00:14.781 --> 00:00:17.022
So today, we're going to
learn from Mark Zuckerberg

00:00:17.022 --> 00:00:18.517
on his Facebook story.

00:00:18.517 --> 00:00:19.583
Mentor me, Mark.

00:00:19.583 --> 00:00:21.694
And as always, guys, as
you're watching the videos,

00:00:21.694 --> 00:00:24.046
if you see something that
really resonates with you,

00:00:24.046 --> 00:00:25.473
please leave it in the comments below

00:00:25.473 --> 00:00:28.253
and put quotes around it so
other people can be inspired,

00:00:28.253 --> 00:00:30.231
and if you write it down,
it's much more likely

00:00:30.231 --> 00:00:32.447
to stick with yourself as well.

00:00:32.447 --> 00:00:35.364
(orchestral music)

00:00:45.663 --> 00:00:47.269
- I built the first version of Facebook

00:00:47.269 --> 00:00:50.032
because it's something
that my friends and I

00:00:50.032 --> 00:00:54.002
wanted to use at Harvard, a
directory and a way to connect

00:00:54.002 --> 00:00:56.659
with the other people around us.

00:00:56.659 --> 00:00:58.582
I didn't think at all that
it was going to be a company.

00:00:58.582 --> 00:01:00.756
I remember very specifically

00:01:00.756 --> 00:01:04.698
the night that we launched
the first version.

00:01:04.698 --> 00:01:08.865
I went out to get pizza
with a couple of my friends

00:01:11.153 --> 00:01:12.653
who now work here.

00:01:14.453 --> 00:01:16.192
I remember really clearly
we were talking about

00:01:16.192 --> 00:01:19.318
how one day, we thought that
someone was going to build

00:01:19.318 --> 00:01:21.558
a community like this for the world,

00:01:21.558 --> 00:01:23.723
and that that would be some company,

00:01:23.723 --> 00:01:26.974
but it clearly wasn't going to be us.

00:01:26.974 --> 00:01:27.807
It wasn't even--

00:01:27.807 --> 00:01:29.219
- It didn't even occur.

00:01:29.219 --> 00:01:30.275
- Yeah.

00:01:30.275 --> 00:01:31.998
It wasn't even an option
that we considered

00:01:31.998 --> 00:01:33.269
that it might be us.

00:01:33.269 --> 00:01:36.388
We just weren't focused on
building a company back then.

00:01:36.388 --> 00:01:38.227
We were just building something

00:01:38.227 --> 00:01:41.191
that we thought would
be useful at our school.

00:01:41.191 --> 00:01:44.476
With Facebook, there was
just such, people loved it,

00:01:44.476 --> 00:01:46.484
and had such an intensity of using it.

00:01:46.484 --> 00:01:48.956
I think within a couple of weeks,

00:01:48.956 --> 00:01:51.460
two thirds of students
at Harvard were using it,

00:01:51.460 --> 00:01:54.276
and all these other students at MIT

00:01:54.276 --> 00:01:57.507
and other local universities
were writing in,

00:01:57.507 --> 00:02:00.520
asking us if we could open
up Facebook at their school.

00:02:00.520 --> 00:02:02.448
So we kind of just followed that.

00:02:02.448 --> 00:02:06.020
Again, I didn't set out and
my roommates didn't set out

00:02:06.020 --> 00:02:08.735
to build a service that we were
going to turn into a company,

00:02:08.735 --> 00:02:10.236
but we just followed what people wanted,

00:02:10.236 --> 00:02:14.152
and that led us to expand it
to all these other schools

00:02:14.152 --> 00:02:16.948
and eventually beyond schools.

00:02:16.948 --> 00:02:19.186
At some point, once we'd
hired a bunch of people,

00:02:19.186 --> 00:02:22.127
we decided to turn it into a company

00:02:22.127 --> 00:02:24.256
and go for this mission
of connecting the world,

00:02:24.256 --> 00:02:25.541
but that's not where we started.

00:02:25.541 --> 00:02:28.791
I think one of the hardest parts for me

00:02:31.871 --> 00:02:36.666
was actually when Yahoo
offered to buy the company

00:02:36.666 --> 00:02:39.010
for a lot of money, because
up until that point,

00:02:39.010 --> 00:02:40.770
that was the turning point in the company

00:02:40.770 --> 00:02:42.270
where before that,

00:02:44.120 --> 00:02:45.284
every day we'd just come in

00:02:45.284 --> 00:02:48.434
and do what we thought was
the right next thing to do.

00:02:48.434 --> 00:02:50.455
We'd open to more schools.

00:02:50.455 --> 00:02:52.946
We opened to high school
and opened beyond schools

00:02:52.946 --> 00:02:57.113
and launched more photos,
'cause that's what seemed like

00:02:58.187 --> 00:02:59.470
the next thing that we needed to do

00:02:59.470 --> 00:03:00.980
to help people express themselves

00:03:00.980 --> 00:03:03.424
and understand more what
was going on around them.

00:03:03.424 --> 00:03:07.591
But then, Yahoo came in with
this really meaningful offer,

00:03:08.897 --> 00:03:10.661
a billion dollars for--

00:03:10.661 --> 00:03:12.244
- And this was how far into the company?

00:03:12.244 --> 00:03:15.486
- It was a couple of years in.

00:03:15.486 --> 00:03:18.253
We had 10 million people
using the product at the time,

00:03:18.253 --> 00:03:21.391
so it wasn't as if it were obvious

00:03:21.391 --> 00:03:24.220
that we were going to
succeed far beyond that,

00:03:24.220 --> 00:03:26.016
and that was the first
point where we really

00:03:26.016 --> 00:03:28.946
had to look at the future and say,

00:03:28.946 --> 00:03:31.891
"Wow, is what we're going to build

00:03:31.891 --> 00:03:36.058
"going to actually be so much
more meaningful for this?

00:03:37.527 --> 00:03:39.507
That caused a lot of
interesting conversations

00:03:39.507 --> 00:03:41.434
in the company and with our investors,

00:03:41.434 --> 00:03:44.682
and at the end of that,
Dustin and I just decided,

00:03:44.682 --> 00:03:46.816
"No, we think that we
can actually go connect

00:03:46.816 --> 00:03:49.200
"more than just the 10 million
people who are in schools.

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:50.650
"We can go beyond that

00:03:50.650 --> 00:03:52.542
"and have this really
be a successful thing."

00:03:52.542 --> 00:03:54.039
And we decided to go for it,
but it was really stressful,

00:03:54.039 --> 00:03:55.260
'cause a lot of people really thought

00:03:55.260 --> 00:03:58.154
that we should sell the company.

00:03:58.154 --> 00:04:00.670
For a lot of folks who joined a startup,

00:04:00.670 --> 00:04:03.250
I feel like at that point
I hadn't been very good

00:04:03.250 --> 00:04:05.215
about communicating that we were

00:04:05.215 --> 00:04:07.482
trying to go for this mission.

00:04:07.482 --> 00:04:08.819
We just showed up every day

00:04:08.819 --> 00:04:12.207
and just did what we thought
was the right next thing to do.

00:04:12.207 --> 00:04:14.439
So for a lot of the folks
who joined early on,

00:04:14.439 --> 00:04:16.227
they weren't really aligned with me.

00:04:16.227 --> 00:04:20.628
For them, they joined, and
being able to sell a company

00:04:20.628 --> 00:04:23.586
for a billion dollars
after a couple years,

00:04:23.586 --> 00:04:25.261
that was like a home run.

00:04:25.261 --> 00:04:27.011
And it is a home run.

00:04:28.897 --> 00:04:32.630
I get that, but I think that the fact that

00:04:32.630 --> 00:04:34.055
I didn't communicate very well

00:04:34.055 --> 00:04:37.711
about what we were trying to
do caused this huge tension.

00:04:37.711 --> 00:04:40.752
And the part that was painful
wasn't turning down the offer.

00:04:40.752 --> 00:04:42.953
It was the fact that after that,

00:04:42.953 --> 00:04:44.216
huge amounts of the company quit

00:04:44.216 --> 00:04:47.225
because they didn't believe
in what we were doing.

00:04:47.225 --> 00:04:49.754
If you look at the
management team that we had--

00:04:49.754 --> 00:04:50.883
- [Interviewer] Did that
whole management team leave?

00:04:50.883 --> 00:04:51.968
- The whole management team.

00:04:51.968 --> 00:04:55.894
It was gone within
about a year after that.

00:04:55.894 --> 00:04:58.786
- Did you ever regret that
decision in that period?

00:04:58.786 --> 00:04:59.740
Were there times where you were like,

00:04:59.740 --> 00:05:02.631
"Well, we should've sold."

00:05:02.631 --> 00:05:03.723
- I got really lucky,

00:05:03.723 --> 00:05:08.390
because not only did what I
believed in end up working out,

00:05:08.390 --> 00:05:10.202
but it ended up working
out pretty quickly.

00:05:10.202 --> 00:05:13.861
So literally, I think this
was in the summer of 2006,

00:05:13.861 --> 00:05:17.278
and by, I think the next month after that

00:05:18.687 --> 00:05:20.788
we launched News Feed,
which now 10 years later,

00:05:20.788 --> 00:05:24.403
looking back at it is one
of the most used products

00:05:24.403 --> 00:05:26.745
in the world, and then we launched

00:05:26.745 --> 00:05:28.497
the ability for anyone to sign up,

00:05:28.497 --> 00:05:32.246
which immediately started
growing the community.

00:05:32.246 --> 00:05:35.036
So within a few months after
turning down the offer,

00:05:35.036 --> 00:05:36.622
I think it was actually pretty clear

00:05:36.622 --> 00:05:38.278
that it was the right decision.

00:05:38.278 --> 00:05:40.424
But I think since then, there have been

00:05:40.424 --> 00:05:41.735
much harder to decisions
that we've had to make

00:05:41.735 --> 00:05:44.767
where sometimes you
have to bet on something

00:05:44.767 --> 00:05:47.982
and either bet the
direction of the company

00:05:47.982 --> 00:05:50.929
or bet billions of dollars on something,

00:05:50.929 --> 00:05:52.264
and it's not going to be
clear whether you're right

00:05:52.264 --> 00:05:54.996
for five or 10 years, and
that, I think, actually,

00:05:54.996 --> 00:05:56.388
can end up being much
harder than this one.

00:05:56.388 --> 00:05:57.478
- That's what I want to talk about next,

00:05:57.478 --> 00:05:58.709
but before we go there,

00:05:58.709 --> 00:06:01.709
have you ever thought about
selling the company again since?

00:06:01.709 --> 00:06:02.542
- No.

00:06:02.542 --> 00:06:03.375
After that point--

00:06:03.375 --> 00:06:04.693
- So that was just--
- Yeah.

00:06:04.693 --> 00:06:05.526
- Got that out of the way.

00:06:05.526 --> 00:06:07.769
We're going to hire people that
want to be here for a long time.

00:06:07.769 --> 00:06:08.602
- Yeah.

00:06:08.602 --> 00:06:09.807
I always think that you should start

00:06:09.807 --> 00:06:13.079
with the problem that you're
trying to solve in the world

00:06:13.079 --> 00:06:16.834
and not start with deciding that
you want to build a company.

00:06:16.834 --> 00:06:19.214
I mean, the best companies that get built

00:06:19.214 --> 00:06:20.996
are things that are trying to drive

00:06:20.996 --> 00:06:22.528
some kind of social change,

00:06:22.528 --> 00:06:25.983
even if it's just local in one place,

00:06:25.983 --> 00:06:26.972
more than starting out

00:06:26.972 --> 00:06:28.804
because you want to make a bunch of money

00:06:28.804 --> 00:06:31.078
or have a lot of people working for you

00:06:31.078 --> 00:06:32.490
or build some company in some way.

00:06:32.490 --> 00:06:34.230
So I always think that this is

00:06:34.230 --> 00:06:37.256
kind of a perverse thing
about Silicon Valley in a way,

00:06:37.256 --> 00:06:39.874
which is that people decide often

00:06:39.874 --> 00:06:41.249
that they want to start a company

00:06:41.249 --> 00:06:43.070
before they even decide
what they want to do.

00:06:43.070 --> 00:06:45.707
And that just feels
really backwards to me.

00:06:45.707 --> 00:06:47.551
And for anyone who's had the experience

00:06:47.551 --> 00:06:49.561
of actually building a company,
you know that you go through

00:06:49.561 --> 00:06:51.300
some really hard things along the way,

00:06:51.300 --> 00:06:54.186
and I think part of what
gets you through that

00:06:54.186 --> 00:06:56.270
is believing in what you're doing

00:06:56.270 --> 00:06:58.560
and knowing that what you're doing

00:06:58.560 --> 00:07:03.168
is really delivering a
lot of value for people,

00:07:03.168 --> 00:07:04.780
and that's, I think,
how the best companies

00:07:04.780 --> 00:07:06.474
end up getting made.

00:07:06.474 --> 00:07:07.783
- Actually, I spend a bunch of time

00:07:07.783 --> 00:07:10.728
analyzing and reflecting on why it was

00:07:10.728 --> 00:07:14.061
that we were even able to do it, because

00:07:14.920 --> 00:07:16.246
all reasons suggest that

00:07:16.246 --> 00:07:17.584
we shouldn't have been able to do it,

00:07:17.584 --> 00:07:18.900
because all these other companies

00:07:18.900 --> 00:07:21.483
had way more engineering power,

00:07:22.674 --> 00:07:25.505
and servers and time and
money and all this stuff.

00:07:25.505 --> 00:07:28.191
And I actually think that this
is a pretty instructive thing

00:07:28.191 --> 00:07:29.520
for anything that you want to go do,

00:07:29.520 --> 00:07:32.002
because the same property
is going to be true

00:07:32.002 --> 00:07:33.274
for anything that you guys start,

00:07:33.274 --> 00:07:35.217
is that someone else is
going to have more resources

00:07:35.217 --> 00:07:36.201
and be able to do it.

00:07:36.201 --> 00:07:37.421
The reason why I think we actually

00:07:37.421 --> 00:07:39.421
ended up being the ones
doing it is because

00:07:39.421 --> 00:07:42.031
we just cared way more
about it than everyone else.

00:07:42.031 --> 00:07:43.976
So there were always projects

00:07:43.976 --> 00:07:46.960
at some of these other companies
that were these hobbies,

00:07:46.960 --> 00:07:49.367
but we always thought that it was

00:07:49.367 --> 00:07:50.386
this really important thing

00:07:50.386 --> 00:07:52.532
and really just felt in
our gut and our heart

00:07:52.532 --> 00:07:54.555
that we wanted to do it.

00:07:54.555 --> 00:07:56.983
Early on, there were always
these skeptics saying that,

00:07:56.983 --> 00:07:58.532
"Oh, this can't be a business."

00:07:58.532 --> 00:07:59.495
We didn't actually care that much

00:07:59.495 --> 00:08:01.290
about it being a business early on.

00:08:01.290 --> 00:08:02.962
But a lot of the reason

00:08:02.962 --> 00:08:04.539
why bigger companies didn't invest in it

00:08:04.539 --> 00:08:05.624
was because it wasn't clear

00:08:05.624 --> 00:08:07.228
that there was a model
that would work for it.

00:08:07.228 --> 00:08:08.548
- [Interviewer] It seemed like a bad idea.

00:08:08.548 --> 00:08:09.464
- Yeah.

00:08:09.464 --> 00:08:10.546
I actually think that that's true

00:08:10.546 --> 00:08:12.288
for a lot of the best ideas,

00:08:12.288 --> 00:08:14.533
where it is that it's not
that someone else can't do it.

00:08:14.533 --> 00:08:17.199
They actually can, and the
odds are stacked against you,

00:08:17.199 --> 00:08:19.393
but I think often that
belief and the fact that

00:08:19.393 --> 00:08:21.716
you just care so much
about what you're doing

00:08:21.716 --> 00:08:24.250
is the only thing that
drives you to do it.

00:08:24.250 --> 00:08:26.760
And to be honest, that kind
of drives me to this day.

00:08:26.760 --> 00:08:29.243
I mean, one of the big emphasis points

00:08:29.243 --> 00:08:32.250
for the company right now is Internet.org,

00:08:32.250 --> 00:08:34.479
and for a while we had
this rallying cry of,

00:08:34.479 --> 00:08:36.702
"Can we connect a billion people?"

00:08:36.702 --> 00:08:38.299
And when we started talking about that,

00:08:38.299 --> 00:08:39.438
we thought that was crazy.

00:08:39.438 --> 00:08:41.945
It was bigger than any
service in the world

00:08:41.945 --> 00:08:45.862
that had been built, and
it was 10 digits long.

00:08:46.759 --> 00:08:48.046
It just felt crazy.

00:08:48.046 --> 00:08:49.177
We'd never get to that.

00:08:49.177 --> 00:08:50.184
But then the thing is,

00:08:50.184 --> 00:08:52.451
as we started to actually
get closer to that,

00:08:52.451 --> 00:08:53.304
we took a step back, and we were like,

00:08:53.304 --> 00:08:54.607
"All right, well our
mission isn't actually

00:08:54.607 --> 00:08:57.193
"to get one in seven people
in the world to be connected.

00:08:57.193 --> 00:08:58.228
"It's we want to connect everyone."

00:08:58.228 --> 00:09:00.487
So it's a big issue that only around

00:09:00.487 --> 00:09:01.757
a third of the people in the world

00:09:01.757 --> 00:09:03.284
have access to the Internet,

00:09:03.284 --> 00:09:04.301
and that's something that we think

00:09:04.301 --> 00:09:05.512
that we can do something about.

00:09:05.512 --> 00:09:07.789
And similar to early Facebook,

00:09:07.789 --> 00:09:09.493
there's no business model around this.

00:09:09.493 --> 00:09:11.774
All the people who have
all the money in the world,

00:09:11.774 --> 00:09:13.220
it's not necessarily a fair thing,

00:09:13.220 --> 00:09:15.224
are already the people
who are on Facebook,

00:09:15.224 --> 00:09:17.933
it's in the first seventh of the world.

00:09:17.933 --> 00:09:19.686
But we just believe really strongly.

00:09:19.686 --> 00:09:21.967
It's like this is what we are here to do.

00:09:21.967 --> 00:09:23.958
This is what our company cares about.

00:09:23.958 --> 00:09:24.791
I care about it.

00:09:24.791 --> 00:09:25.624
The team cares about it.

00:09:25.624 --> 00:09:26.486
Our culture cares about it.

00:09:26.486 --> 00:09:28.528
So we're just going to keep pushing on it.

00:09:28.528 --> 00:09:29.918
And I actually think a lot of the reason

00:09:29.918 --> 00:09:31.947
why great stuff gets built is because

00:09:31.947 --> 00:09:35.673
it's kind of irrational
at the time, so it selects

00:09:35.673 --> 00:09:38.024
for the people who care
the most about it doing it.

00:09:38.024 --> 00:09:39.966
We had a very simple focus and idea.

00:09:39.966 --> 00:09:42.541
The goal wasn't to make
a huge community site.

00:09:42.541 --> 00:09:43.446
It was to make something

00:09:43.446 --> 00:09:44.951
where you could type in someone's name

00:09:44.951 --> 00:09:46.775
and find out a bunch of
information about them.

00:09:46.775 --> 00:09:48.270
(synthesizer music)

00:09:48.270 --> 00:09:50.926
So I took a few days and
just threw together Facebook

00:09:50.926 --> 00:09:53.273
and launched it on February 4th, 2004.

00:09:53.273 --> 00:09:54.935
It was just me working on it at that time,

00:09:54.935 --> 00:09:56.992
but at that point, my roommate Dustin,

00:09:56.992 --> 00:09:59.474
who hadn't had much computer
programming experience at all,

00:09:59.474 --> 00:10:01.461
was like, "I can help you expand it,"

00:10:01.461 --> 00:10:02.413
and I'm like, "That's cool, dude,

00:10:02.413 --> 00:10:04.972
"but you don't know how
to program." (laughs)

00:10:04.972 --> 00:10:07.514
So he's like, "No, no, it's not an issue."

00:10:07.514 --> 00:10:10.252
He went home one weekend, bought
the book Perl for Dummies,

00:10:10.252 --> 00:10:11.699
came back and was like,
"All right, I'm ready."

00:10:11.699 --> 00:10:12.909
And then we started getting emails

00:10:12.909 --> 00:10:14.257
from people at a bunch of other schools

00:10:14.257 --> 00:10:16.243
asking if we could launch Facebooks there.

00:10:16.243 --> 00:10:19.006
In the last tech bubble,
most websites were run

00:10:19.006 --> 00:10:21.427
using these really expensive machines,

00:10:21.427 --> 00:10:24.943
which made it that you had to
basically go and raise money

00:10:24.943 --> 00:10:26.207
before you could do anything.

00:10:26.207 --> 00:10:28.879
We ran the site originally
for 85 dollars a month,

00:10:28.879 --> 00:10:30.962
renting computers for
the first three months.

00:10:30.962 --> 00:10:33.925
- [Interviewer] Were you ever in debt?

00:10:33.925 --> 00:10:37.179
- I was in debt 160 dollars. (laughs)

00:10:37.179 --> 00:10:40.855
In a world that's changing so quickly,

00:10:40.855 --> 00:10:45.022
the biggest risk you can
take is not taking any risk.

00:10:46.723 --> 00:10:48.511
I really think that that's true.

00:10:48.511 --> 00:10:51.761
A lot of people, I think, think that...

00:10:53.020 --> 00:10:54.444
Whenever you get yourself into a position

00:10:54.444 --> 00:10:56.699
where you have to make some big shift

00:10:56.699 --> 00:11:01.429
in direction or do something,
people are going to point

00:11:01.429 --> 00:11:04.952
to the downside risks of that decision.

00:11:04.952 --> 00:11:07.202
Locally, they may be right.

00:11:08.280 --> 00:11:09.928
For any given decision
that you're going to make,

00:11:09.928 --> 00:11:11.920
there's upside and downside.

00:11:11.920 --> 00:11:14.754
But in aggregate, if you are stagnant

00:11:14.754 --> 00:11:16.743
and you don't make those changes,

00:11:16.743 --> 00:11:20.777
then I think you're guaranteed
to fail and not catch up.

00:11:20.777 --> 00:11:23.215
So to some degree, I
think it's really right

00:11:23.215 --> 00:11:26.503
that over time the biggest
risk that you can take

00:11:26.503 --> 00:11:28.515
is to not take any risks.

00:11:28.515 --> 00:11:31.706
Now we've made a huge number
of mistakes along the way.

00:11:31.706 --> 00:11:33.216
I always say, I just think we've made

00:11:33.216 --> 00:11:35.452
probably every possible
mistake you could make.

00:11:35.452 --> 00:11:38.041
- Is there a decision that you've made

00:11:38.041 --> 00:11:42.778
that the people around
you told you was a mistake

00:11:42.778 --> 00:11:45.772
and you defied them and you were right?

00:11:45.772 --> 00:11:47.255
- The most famous one, I think,

00:11:47.255 --> 00:11:49.764
probably has to do with
selling the company.

00:11:49.764 --> 00:11:52.426
In 2006, we had this opportunity

00:11:52.426 --> 00:11:55.466
to sell the company to
Yahoo for a billion dollars,

00:11:55.466 --> 00:11:57.422
and we turned that down.

00:11:57.422 --> 00:11:58.710
I think a lot of people at the time

00:11:58.710 --> 00:12:00.423
thought that we should sell the company,

00:12:00.423 --> 00:12:02.673
but I felt really strongly.

00:12:04.212 --> 00:12:06.279
I think now people generally think that

00:12:06.279 --> 00:12:07.506
that was a good decision.

00:12:07.506 --> 00:12:10.430
A lot of people who I think
build startups or companies

00:12:10.430 --> 00:12:12.749
think that selling the
company or going public

00:12:12.749 --> 00:12:13.931
is this end point.

00:12:13.931 --> 00:12:16.476
It's like you win when you go public.

00:12:16.476 --> 00:12:18.255
That's just not how I see it.

00:12:18.255 --> 00:12:19.088
- [Interviewer] What do you think

00:12:19.088 --> 00:12:20.046
that Facebook brings to the table

00:12:20.046 --> 00:12:23.687
that other online communities don't have?

00:12:23.687 --> 00:12:25.991
- I think that the fact that Facebook

00:12:25.991 --> 00:12:28.169
is tied into the real world

00:12:28.169 --> 00:12:30.697
instead of just being an online community

00:12:30.697 --> 00:12:32.042
is the big difference.

00:12:32.042 --> 00:12:34.546
So a lot of online communities

00:12:34.546 --> 00:12:36.961
don't really have a set culture,

00:12:36.961 --> 00:12:39.688
so people don't know how to act online.

00:12:39.688 --> 00:12:41.264
Right now, we're face to face

00:12:41.264 --> 00:12:42.733
and we're acting in a certain way,

00:12:42.733 --> 00:12:44.521
which is different than we'd act

00:12:44.521 --> 00:12:48.688
if you were just a screen name
on my monitor or something.

00:12:50.503 --> 00:12:53.670
The fact that this is tied to culture,

00:12:55.409 --> 00:12:59.748
my friends at Harvard will
observe how I act on Facebook,

00:12:59.748 --> 00:13:02.020
makes it a different kind of community,

00:13:02.020 --> 00:13:04.226
because it's actually an online community

00:13:04.226 --> 00:13:06.428
with the values of a real community.

00:13:06.428 --> 00:13:08.072
So I think that makes it cool.

00:13:08.072 --> 00:13:11.732
It makes it so that there's
real life, useful information

00:13:11.732 --> 00:13:14.482
that's available there,
and the interactions

00:13:14.482 --> 00:13:17.240
also sometimes are more
applicable to real life, too.

00:13:17.240 --> 00:13:20.740
So I think that that's pretty interesting.

00:13:21.741 --> 00:13:23.921
There's also cool interactions

00:13:23.921 --> 00:13:27.965
that occur on other sites,
but this is very real, I feel,

00:13:27.965 --> 00:13:31.182
and I think that's what
we're going for with it.

00:13:31.182 --> 00:13:34.465
- Do you think there's anything about you,

00:13:34.465 --> 00:13:36.522
like a personal quality of yours

00:13:36.522 --> 00:13:39.435
besides basic smartness and determination,

00:13:39.435 --> 00:13:42.942
that made you well suited
to work on this project?

00:13:42.942 --> 00:13:45.913
I realize this requires
some introspection.

00:13:45.913 --> 00:13:46.746
- Yeah.

00:13:46.746 --> 00:13:47.659
I actually think determination

00:13:47.659 --> 00:13:50.942
is probably the biggest piece.

00:13:50.942 --> 00:13:53.658
So many things go wrong when
you're starting a company,

00:13:53.658 --> 00:13:55.537
and often, I think people ask,

00:13:55.537 --> 00:13:58.957
"What mistakes should you avoid making?"

00:13:58.957 --> 00:14:01.260
My answer to that question
is don't even bother

00:14:01.260 --> 00:14:02.364
trying to avoid mistakes,

00:14:02.364 --> 00:14:05.688
because you're going to
make tons of mistakes.

00:14:05.688 --> 00:14:07.772
The important thing is
actually learning quickly

00:14:07.772 --> 00:14:11.427
from whatever mistakes you
make and not giving up.

00:14:11.427 --> 00:14:14.546
There are things every single
year of Facebook's existence

00:14:14.546 --> 00:14:16.462
that could've killed us or made it so that

00:14:16.462 --> 00:14:17.773
it just seemed like moving forward

00:14:17.773 --> 00:14:20.183
and making a lot of progress
just seemed intractable,

00:14:20.183 --> 00:14:22.988
but you just bounce back and you learn.

00:14:22.988 --> 00:14:24.245
Nothing is impossible.

00:14:24.245 --> 00:14:27.983
You just have to keep
running through the walls.

00:14:27.983 --> 00:14:29.203
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:14:29.203 --> 00:14:30.214
I hope you enjoyed.

00:14:30.214 --> 00:14:32.473
I'd love to know, what
did you learn from Mark

00:14:32.473 --> 00:14:33.911
that you can immediately apply

00:14:33.911 --> 00:14:35.697
to your life or business somehow?

00:14:35.697 --> 00:14:36.977
Leave it down in the comments below,

00:14:36.977 --> 00:14:38.759
and I'm going to join in the discussion.

00:14:38.759 --> 00:14:40.948
I also want to give a quick
shoutout to Katie Kruse.

00:14:40.948 --> 00:14:42.486
Katie, thank you so much
for picking up a copy

00:14:42.486 --> 00:14:45.286
of my book, Your One Word,
and making that awesome

00:14:45.286 --> 00:14:48.178
animated YouTube summary
of it on your channel.

00:14:48.178 --> 00:14:50.499
Thank you, thank you,
thank you for your support.

00:14:50.499 --> 00:14:52.813
- [Narrator] We are talking
about Your One Word,

00:14:52.813 --> 00:14:54.196
the powerful secret to creating

00:14:54.196 --> 00:14:57.750
a business and life that
matters by Evan Carmichael.

00:14:57.750 --> 00:14:59.224
So thank you guys again for watching.

00:14:59.224 --> 00:15:00.216
I believe in you.

00:15:00.216 --> 00:15:02.221
I hope you continue to believe in yourself

00:15:02.221 --> 00:15:04.385
and whatever your one word is.

00:15:04.385 --> 00:15:05.454
Much love.

00:15:05.454 --> 00:15:07.005
I'll see you soon.

00:15:07.005 --> 00:15:09.452
(loud whoosh)

